  { id:'opmcq-c9-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"A 70-year-old man presents with a white, opaque lens and cannot see the chart. He can only detect hand movements close to his face. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Nuclear sclerosis","Posterior subcapsular cataract","Mature cataract","Corneal edema"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Two facts are handed over and they are the same two the deck uses to define maturity: a totally white lens and vision of hand movements.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 7 \"Classification by Maturity\" gives the mature stage as:\n\n> **VA: HM only (not less!)** · **Red Reflex: ABSENT** · Totally opaque white lens. Shadow test: NEGATIVE. Good projection + colour discrimination = good prognosis\n\nand `L17) Cataract I` says the same twice — morphologically \"Mature → **complete** opacification of lens cortex & nucleus\", and among the signs \"Mature cataract → HM\". `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` states the pathology in one line: \"A cataract with a clear cortex remaining is immature. **A mature cataract has a totally opacified cortex.**\"\n\n**The 'not less than HM' clause is the part worth carrying.** `L18` slide 4 prints it as a Key Clinical Principle — \"Cataract alone (without posterior segment disease) should **NEVER** reduce vision to less than Hand Motion (HM). VA < HM → suspect concurrent retinal or optic nerve pathology\" — and `L17` repeats it as \"Never PL or No PL due to cataract **or any media opacity**\". So HM is not an incidental detail in this stem; it is the ceiling that says the lens alone accounts for the loss.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Nuclear sclerosis** | The normal ageing change, not a diagnosis of blindness. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.10 defines it: the pupil is \"jet black in the newly born and starts to acquire a **greyish tinge** as the person gets older due to the continuous addition of new lens fibres throughout life causing light scattering; a process known as **senile sclerosis**\". Greyish, not white; and `L18` slide 6 makes nuclear *cataract* the \"**exaggeration** of nuclear sclerosis\". This chapter asks the distinction head-on at `opmcq-c9-9` |\n| **B. Posterior subcapsular cataract** | A plaque at the posterior capsule only — `L18` slide 8 gives it a \"granular posterior capsule plaque\" on slit lamp and a \"dark plaque at posterior pole\" against the red reflex. The lens as a whole stays clear, so it can never look white and opaque, and its signature complaint is near vision in bright light, not hand movements |\n| **C. Mature cataract** | The key |\n| **D. Corneal edema** | Not a lens opacity at all, and the stem has already told you the **lens** is white and opaque. A hazy cornea would be described at the cornea |\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain works the same picture from a photograph — `opqb-t9-368` shows a pupil filled edge to edge with dense uniform white-grey material and asks the effect on vision, keying **VA less than CF**. Same lens, opposite direction of travel.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A totally white opaque lens with vision of hand movements is a mature cataract; HM is the floor a media opacity can reach, and anything worse means coexisting retinal or optic nerve disease.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.62 (Ch.9 Q1, the first question of the chapter)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"A 70-year-old man reports that he can suddenly read without his glasses, even though his distance vision has worsened. He denies eye pain. Slit-lamp exam shows yellowing and hardening of the central part of the lens. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Posterior subcapsular cataract","Cortical cataract","Nuclear cataract","Mature cataract"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The slit-lamp line and the symptom line are the two halves of one slide.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 6 \"Classification by Morphology\", nuclear column:\n\n> Exaggeration of nuclear sclerosis (**yellowing → brunescence**) · Increased nuclear refractive index → **acquired myopia** · Interferes **predominantly with distant vision** · **'Second sight': elderly regain near vision temporarily** · Red reflex: central distortion / dark nucleus\n\nYellowing of the nucleus is the sign; reading without glasses while distance vision falls away is the symptom; and the arrow between them is the refractive index. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` states the mechanism in the same words from the other direction: \"Patients with **nuclear sclerosis** may develop increasing **lenticular myopia** because of the **increased refractive power of the denser nucleus**.\" `L17) Cataract I` supplies the colour sequence — \"Nuclear → grey, yellow brown, black\".\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Posterior subcapsular cataract** | ⚠️ **The trap, and it fails on the direction of the change.** PSC does single out near vision — `L18` slide 8 gives it \"Near worse (miosis aggravates)\" and \"Reading & bright sunlight\" as the most troubled task — but it makes near vision **worse**, not better, and it sits at the posterior capsule, not \"the central part of the lens\" |\n| **B. Cortical cataract** | \"Opacification of lens fibres **surrounding** the nucleus\", beginning as \"wedge-shaped / spoke-like **peripheral** opacities\" (`L18` slide 6). Peripheral, not central; and its hallmark is glare, with the effect on acuity depending on how close the spokes come to the visual axis |\n| **C. Nuclear cataract** | The key |\n| **D. Mature cataract** | A stage, not a morphology, and the wrong stage: `L18` slide 7 requires a \"totally opaque white lens\" with VA down to HM. This man still reads. That picture is `opmcq-c9-1` |\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain runs the identical inference twice without ever showing the slit lamp — `opqb-t9-398` (a 45-year-old emmetropic driver who can read without glasses but cannot see far street signs) and `opqb-t9-407` (a 60-year-old originally emmetropic with defective far vision and good reading without his presbyopic correction), both keyed **nuclear cataract**. Neither states the yellow nucleus, which is what this stem adds.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A yellowing, hardening nucleus with better near vision and worse distance vision is nuclear cataract — the denser nucleus raises the refractive index and induces myopia; PSC also targets near vision but degrades it.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.62 (Ch.9 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"A 52-year-old man tells his ophthalmologist that he no longer needs the reading glasses as he can now see the near objects more clearly than ever. How should the ophthalmologist react to this data?",
    options:["It is insignificant; so he has to ignore it","It is a good new; so he has to congratulate the patient","It may be a bad news; so he has to further evaluate the patient","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ *Options B and C are printed exactly as transcribed — \"a good new\" and \"a bad news\" are the bank's, verified at 400 dpi.*\n\n**The course files recovered near vision under SYMPTOMS, which settles the question before any reasoning starts.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 5 \"Senile Cataract — Symptoms & Signs\" lists, in the symptom column alongside gradual visual loss, glare and monocular diplopia:\n\n> **'Second sight'** — Nuclear: myopic shift allows reading without glasses (**temporary**)\n\nA symptom is a complaint about a disease. The word *temporary* is the second half of the warning: the myopic shift that hands back near vision is produced by a nucleus that is still hardening, so the improvement is a waypoint on a curve that ends in `opmcq-c9-1`.\n\n**The age fits and it is not incidental.** `L17) Cataract I` prints \"Senile cataract starts from the **5th decade** onwards, with no gender or laterality predilection\" — 52 is exactly the window in which this story becomes suspicious rather than lucky. It is also the age at which presbyopia is established, which is why losing the need for reading glasses at 52 is abnormal rather than a late maturation.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. It is insignificant; so he has to ignore it** | It is a printed symptom of a named disease. Ignoring it forfeits the chance to document the lens now and to explain to the patient why his distance vision will follow |\n| **B. It is a good new; so he has to congratulate the patient** | ⚠️ **The humane wrong answer.** The patient has genuinely gained something, and it will be taken back — `L18` calls the gain *temporary*, and `L18` slide 6 has \"interferes predominantly with distant vision\" running underneath the whole time |\n| **C. It may be a bad news; so he has to further evaluate the patient** | The key. Evaluation means the slit lamp — `L18` slide 20 makes \"Lens: morphology (nuclear/cortical/PSC), maturity\" a step of the structured examination, with **dilation essential** to grade the opacity properly |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails, C being correct |\n\n⚠️ **This is the antecedent of `opmcq-c9-4`**, which is printed as \"In the previous case…\" and never names its patient. Read the two together. The same phenomenon is asked for by name at `opmcq-c9-25` and worked from the other end at `opmcq-c9-2`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Recovered near vision in a middle-aged presbyope is second sight, which the course lists as a SYMPTOM of senile cataract and calls temporary — it is a reason to examine the lens, not to congratulate the patient.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.62 (Ch.9 Q3; options B and C printed "It is a good new" and "It may be a bad news")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"A 52-year-old man no longer needs his reading glasses and says he can see near objects more clearly than ever. What is the most likely explanation for the patient's improvement of vision?",
    options:["The reading glasses made the patient's near vision better and more flexible","The patient was just feeling so but this can't be true","Refractive index of the lens nucleus increased due to cataract","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, what is the most likely explanation for the patient's improvement of vision?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c9-3`, printed immediately above it on book p.62, and it was opened at 400 dpi rather than assumed. The chain is one deep — `opmcq-c9-3` introduces its own patient. The restatement above repeats that man's **complaint** and stops there: it does not say *cataract*, because that is this question's own key, and it does not say *this may be bad news*, because that is `opmcq-c9-3`'s.\n\n**The mechanism is printed as an arrow on one slide and as a sentence on another.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 6, nuclear column: \"**Increased nuclear refractive index → acquired myopia**\", with the deck's anatomy slide 3 giving the baseline it moves from — \"Refractive index: **1.386 (nucleus) — increases with age**\". `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` writes the same thing out: \"Patients with nuclear sclerosis may develop increasing lenticular myopia because of the **increased refractive power of the denser nucleus**.\"\n\nA more powerful lens brings the eye's far point in from infinity to somewhere within arm's reach. Near objects now focus on the retina without accommodation and without a reading add — which is why the near gain and the distance loss are **one event**, not two.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. The reading glasses made the patient's near vision better and more flexible** | Spectacles bend light in front of the eye; they change nothing inside it. Presbyopia is a loss of accommodative amplitude in the lens itself, and wearing an add neither trains it nor restores it |\n| **B. The patient was just feeling so but this can't be true** | The course prints the phenomenon under its own name, as a symptom. Dismissing the history is the failure mode `opmcq-c9-3` keys against |\n| **C. Refractive index of the lens nucleus increased due to cataract** | The key |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n**Cross-bank, and the comparison is instructive.** Grade Gain asks the same mechanism at `opqb-t9-360` — \"the myopic change in the refractive state of the eye in nuclear sclerosis can be attributed to\" — but offers **three** mechanisms (increased lens curvature, anterior displacement of the iris-lens diaphragm, increased refractive index of the nucleus) and keys **all of the above**. House offers one mechanism against two non-answers. The Grade Gain menu is the fuller teaching; this one is the cleaner test.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Second sight is a myopic shift: the sclerosing nucleus raises its refractive index, the eye becomes more powerful, and the near gain and the distance loss are the same event.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.62 (Ch.9 Q4; printed "In the previous case, what is the most likely explanation for the patient\'s improvement of vision?" and refers back to Ch.9 Q3)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"A 66-year-old male patient complaining of recent progressive difficulty in seeing far objects (myopic shift). He reports that he used to see perfectly before. Which of the following might be the cause of that patient's condition?",
    options:["Presbyopia","Glaucoma","Nuclear cataract","Retinitis pigmentosa"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The bank prints the mechanism inside the stem — \"(myopic shift)\" — so the question is really: which of these four causes an acquired myopia in an eye that used to be emmetropic?** Only one does. `L18) Cataract II` slide 6: \"Increased nuclear refractive index → **acquired myopia**\", and `L35,36) Chronic visual loss`: \"Patients with nuclear sclerosis may develop **increasing lenticular myopia** because of the increased refractive power of the denser nucleus.\"\n\n| Option | Why it cannot produce this history |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Presbyopia** | The wrong distance and the wrong direction. Presbyopia is the age-related loss of accommodation, so it takes away **near** vision in a patient whose distance vision is unaffected. It is also universal by 66, not \"recent\" |\n| **B. Glaucoma** | ⚠️ **Worth pausing over, because the deck does give POAG a refractive symptom.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lists the symptoms as \"Most commonly → **asymptomatic** · Less commonly → headache (early morning), **frequent change of reading glasses** · Advanced disease → peripheral visual field loss, diminution of vision\". Changing reading glasses is not a myopic shift, and the visual loss POAG causes is **peripheral field**, arriving late and painlessly — not a progressive blur of distant objects in an eye that saw perfectly a year ago |\n| **C. Nuclear cataract** | The key |\n| **D. Retinitis pigmentosa** | A retinal dystrophy: night blindness and progressive peripheral field constriction, typically presenting decades earlier and never as a change of refraction. ⚠️ Note the real relationship — `L35,36` lists \"**Retinal dystrophy, retinitis pigmentosa**\" among the causes of **complicated cataract**, so RP is upstream of a cataract, not an alternative to one. *(That RP's own symptoms are night blindness and ring field loss is **not taken from the course material**: `retinitis pigmentosa` returns four cached files — `L1,2`, `L35,36`, `book/ch01` and `book/ch14` — and in every one it is a list item, never a described disease.)* |\n\n**Why the answer is not \"mature cataract\" even though it is not offered.** A myopic shift is a property of a lens that is still transmitting light. Once the cortex opacifies completely the refraction stops mattering, which is the point `opmcq-c9-1` and `opmcq-c9-6` make.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t9-402` is the closest Grade Gain question — a 60-year-old previously emmetropic patient with a healthy fundus who develops \"gradual progressive simple myopia\" — and it keys **nuclear cataracts** against cortical cataract, chronic simple glaucoma and keratoconus. Its menu supplies the fourth candidate this one leaves out: keratoconus, which also causes acquired myopia, but by steepening the **cornea** rather than densifying the lens.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"An acquired myopic shift in a previously emmetropic elderly eye points at the nucleus of the lens; presbyopia degrades near vision, POAG takes peripheral field, and retinitis pigmentosa causes cataract rather than refractive change.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.63 (Ch.9 Q5; the stem prints "(myopic shift)" in parentheses)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"A 64-year-old man complains of gradual painless vision impairment. Ophthalmoscopic examination showed absent red reflex. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Nuclear cataract","Cortical cataract","Mature cataract","Posterior subcapsular cataract"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The red reflex is the discriminator, and the deck grades all four stages by it on one slide.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 5 compresses it into a single line under SIGNS — \"Red reflex: **Absent (mature)**, dim (immature), altered (nuclear)\" — and slide 7 lays it out stage by stage:\n\n| Stage | VA | Red reflex |\n|---|---|---|\n| Incipient | 6/18 – 6/6 | Clear centrally, dark periphery |\n| Immature | 6/18 – CF | **Dim** |\n| Mature | **HM only (not less!)** | **ABSENT** |\n| Hypermature | HM or less | Variable |\n\nOnly one row says absent. A totally opaque lens returns no light at all from the fundus, which is the same fact `opmcq-c9-1` states as a white pupil and `opmcq-c9-20` exploits when it asks how to see the retina behind one.\n\n**The three morphological options all leave a red reflex present — they merely put something dark in it.** `L18` slide 8's red-reflex row:\n\n| Option | What its red reflex looks like |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Nuclear cataract** | \"**Dark nucleus centrally**\" — the reflex is there, with the nucleus silhouetted in the middle of it. Slide 6 calls it \"central distortion\". `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 20 goes further and gives nuclear sclerosis a **bright** red reflex against immature senile cataract's dim one |\n| **B. Cortical cataract** | \"**Dark spokes peripherally**\" — the wedge-shaped opacities appear dark against a reflex that is otherwise intact. Slide 6: \"Appear dark against the red reflex\" |\n| **C. Mature cataract** | The key — nothing to appear against |\n| **D. Posterior subcapsular cataract** | \"**Dark plaque at posterior pole**\", seen by retroillumination, i.e. *by* the red reflex |\n\n**\"Gradual painless\" is the other half of the stem and it is doing real work.** `L17) Cataract I` gives the symptom block the heading \"**Gradual progressive painless diminution of vision**\" and its take-home message says cataract is \"the most common cause\" of exactly that. Pain would move the question to the red eye; sudden loss would move it to `op-acute`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"An absent red reflex means the lens transmits nothing — a mature cataract; nuclear, cortical and posterior subcapsular opacities are all seen AGAINST a red reflex that is still present.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.63 (Ch.9 Q6)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"Which of the following is the best treatment option for a patient complaining of diminution of vision 2 years following cataract surgery?",
    options:["Topical steroids","YAG laser capsulotomy","Intraocular lens exchange","Optical coherence tomography (OCT)"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The stem gives one datum — the interval — and the deck's complication slide is organised by interval.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 16 \"Postoperative Complications\" times every entry:\n\n| Complication | When |\n|---|---|\n| **Posterior capsule opacification (PCO)** | **Months–years** |\n| Endophthalmitis | 24–72 hrs (acute) or weeks (delayed) |\n| Cystoid macular oedema | Weeks–months |\n| Corneal decompensation | Early or late |\n| Refractive surprise | Immediate |\n\nTwo years is inside one window only, and PCO is also the one this bank has already keyed as commonest at `opmcq-c9-10`. The slide's PCO cell reads:\n\n> Most common late complication. Lens epithelial cells migrate on the posterior capsule → **'after-cataract'**. Presents as **gradual return of visual blurring**. Tx: **Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy** — outpatient, definitive, excellent outcomes.\n\nThe mechanism is worth holding because it explains why the treatment is a laser and not a drug: phacoemulsification leaves the posterior capsule in place deliberately (`L18` slide 13, ECCE column: \"Posterior capsule **LEFT IN PLACE** (supports IOL)\"), and residual lens epithelial cells creep across it. Nothing is inflamed and nothing is infected — a membrane is simply in the way, so the treatment is to cut a hole in it.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Topical steroids** | Treat inflammation. PCO is cellular migration and fibrosis on a capsule, not iritis; steroids do belong in this chapter, but to cystoid macular oedema — slide 16's CME cell keys \"topical NSAIDs + steroids, sub-Tenon's triamcinolone\" |\n| **B. YAG laser capsulotomy** | The key |\n| **C. Intraocular lens exchange** | ⚠️ Real surgery for a real problem, but the wrong problem. Slide 16 assigns IOL exchange to **refractive surprise** (\"Tx: IOL exchange or piggyback IOL, LASIK enhancement\"), which the same slide times as **immediate** — not two years later. Removing a well-placed IOL for a cloudy capsule behind it would leave the cloudy capsule |\n| **D. Optical coherence tomography (OCT)** | Not a treatment at all, and the stem asks for one. OCT is slide 16's **diagnostic** for cystoid macular oedema (\"Dx: OCT (petaloid fluid pattern)\") and slide 12 lists it in the pre-operative work-up |\n\n**Cross-bank, and the two printings are complementary rather than duplicate.** `opqb-t9-380` gives the same interval story with the sign supplied — \"a **grayish membrane behind the IOL**\" five years on — and keys YAG capsulotomy against IOL removal, surgical exploration and topical steroids. `opqb-t9-349` strips it to vocabulary: which laser is used for capsulotomy, keyed **YAG** against diode, carbon dioxide and excimer. This House printing is the hardest of the three, because it names no finding and expects the interval alone to carry the diagnosis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Blurring that returns months to years after cataract surgery is posterior capsule opacification, and its definitive outpatient treatment is Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy — not steroids, not IOL exchange, and OCT is a test rather than a treatment.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.63 (Ch.9 Q7)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most commonly used surgical technique in order to treat a patient with cataract?",
    options:["Extracapsular cataract extraction","Phacoemulsification","Intracapsular cataract extraction","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The deck answers this in a badge.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 13 \"Cataract Surgery — Techniques\" prints three columns, and the phacoemulsification column carries the label **STANDARD** in the header and closes with the line \"**Gold standard worldwide for routine cataract surgery**\". `L17) Cataract I` gives the same ranking from the operative side: \"Adults → conventional ECCE, **small incision cataract extraction (phacoemulsification)**\".\n\n**What makes it standard is the incision, and everything else follows from it.**\n\n| | Phaco | ECCE | ICCE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Incision | **2.2 – 3.0 mm** clear corneal or scleral tunnel | 8 – 10 mm limbal | Large — whole lens and capsule out |\n| Nucleus | Ultrasonically emulsified in the eye (divide & conquer, stop & chop, phaco chop) | Manually expressed through an anterior capsulotomy | Removed entire, by cryo or forceps |\n| Posterior capsule | Left, IOL in the bag | Left, supports the IOL | **None left** — IOL goes in the anterior chamber |\n| IOL | **Foldable acrylic** through 2.2 mm | Rigid PMMA (does not fold) | ACIOL |\n| Wound | **Self-sealing, no sutures → less astigmatism** | 10/0 nylon sutures → significant astigmatism | — |\n\n| Option | Where the deck puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Extracapsular cataract extraction** | ⚠️ **Not obsolete — the deck keeps it for named indications**: \"Still used for **very hard (grade 4–5) nuclei**, subluxated lenses, resource-limited settings\". It is the fallback, not the default |\n| **B. Phacoemulsification** | The key |\n| **C. Intracapsular cataract extraction** | \"**Historical procedure — rarely performed today**\", with \"high rate of complications: bullous keratopathy, glaucoma, vitreous prolapse\". Slide 13 keeps one live indication — \"Still performed in **Morgagnian cataract with zonular weakness** (careful selection)\", which is `opmcq-c9-19`'s cataract |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n⚠️ **In children the answer would be none of these.** `L17` prescribes \"Children → **irrigation / aspiration** (soft lens)\", because a paediatric lens has no nucleus to emulsify. The stem's \"a patient with cataract\" is read as the adult default the deck itself defaults to.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain never asks which technique is commonest; it asks what commonness buys. `opqb-t9-353` keys phaco + IOL for **earliest visual rehabilitation** and `opqb-t9-381` keys it for the **least postoperative astigmatism** — the two consequences of the self-sealing 2.2 mm wound.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Phacoemulsification is the worldwide standard for routine cataract surgery — a 2.2–3.0 mm self-sealing incision, a foldable IOL in the bag and no sutures; ECCE survives for very hard nuclei and ICCE is historical.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.63 (Ch.9 Q8)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"Which of the following best differentiates nuclear sclerosis from nuclear cataract?",
    options:["Nuclear sclerosis alters red reflex while nuclear cataract doesn't alter it","Nuclear cataract alters red reflex while nuclear sclerosis doesn't alter it","Nuclear sclerosis doesn't affect vision while nuclear cataract affects it","A & B","B & C"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"⚠️ *Option E and the answer line are printed at the head of book p.64; the stem and options A–D sit at the foot of p.63. Both ends of the seam were read.*\n\n**These are two points on one continuum, and the course draws the line in two places — at the red reflex and at the vision.**\n\n**The vision line is the definition itself.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 4: \"CATARACT = **Any opacity of the crystalline lens sufficient to impair vision**\". `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` says the same thing about the reservation of the word: \"Clinically, the term cataract is usually **reserved for opacities that affect visual acuity**, because **many normal lenses have small, visually insignificant opacities**.\" Sclerosis that has not yet cost acuity is not a cataract; when it does, it is — `L18` slide 6 calls nuclear cataract the \"**exaggeration** of nuclear sclerosis\". **That is statement C.**\n\n**The red-reflex line is printed with the two states side by side.** `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 20 photographs three pupils and captions them: \"Black (Newly born & Aphakia)\" · \"Grayish in old age — **Nuclear sclerosis (bright RR)** / **IMSC (Dim RR)**\" · \"Leukocoria (Mature cataract)\". Sclerosis leaves the reflex bright; the cataract dims it, and `L18` slide 8 gives the cataract a \"**dark nucleus centrally**\" against it. **That is statement B.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.10 supplies the reason the sclerotic lens still passes light: the greyish tinge comes from \"the continuous addition of new lens fibres throughout life causing **light scattering**\" — scattering, not blockage.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Nuclear sclerosis alters red reflex while nuclear cataract doesn't** | **B with the two nouns swapped.** The classic reversed-statement distractor: every content word is right and the direction is wrong |\n| **B. Nuclear cataract alters red reflex while nuclear sclerosis doesn't** | True — bright reflex in sclerosis, dark nucleus against a dimmed reflex in cataract |\n| **C. Nuclear sclerosis doesn't affect vision while nuclear cataract affects it** | True — and it is the definition, not a sign |\n| **D. A & B** | Cannot be right: A and B contradict each other, so no answer can contain both |\n| **E. B & C** | The key |\n\n⚠️ **A caution the bank does not print.** Read strictly, statement B is a simplification — `L3,4` gives sclerosis a *bright* reflex rather than an unchanged one, and `L18` slide 5's own summary line says the reflex is \"**altered** (nuclear)\". The two are separated by degree, since one is the exaggeration of the other. The bank's key is nevertheless the intended reading and stands: the reflex is preserved in sclerosis and interrupted in cataract.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Nuclear cataract is nuclear sclerosis exaggerated to the point of impairing vision: sclerosis keeps a bright red reflex and costs no acuity, cataract silhouettes a dark nucleus against a dimmed reflex and does.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.63-64 (Ch.9 Q9; the option list is split across the page break — stem and options A-D on p.63, option E and the key on p.64)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most common postoperative complication following treatment of cataract?",
    options:["Endophthalmitis","Posterior capsular opacification","Recurrence","All of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**`L18) Cataract II` slide 16 ranks these in the first line of each cell, and the two superlatives go to two different complications.**\n\n| Complication | Timing | The deck's own first words |\n|---|---|---|\n| Posterior capsule opacification | Months–years | \"**Most common** late complication\" |\n| Endophthalmitis | 24–72 h acute, weeks delayed | \"**Most serious** vision-threatening complication\" |\n| Cystoid macular oedema | Weeks–months | Irvine-Gass syndrome |\n| Corneal decompensation | Early or late | Bullous keratopathy from endothelial cell loss |\n| Refractive surprise | Immediate | Biometry inaccuracy |\n\nPCO happens because the operation deliberately leaves the posterior capsule behind to hold the IOL, and residual **lens epithelial cells migrate across it** — the deck's word for the result is \"**after-cataract**\". Nothing has gone wrong; the commonest late complication of the standard operation is a consequence of how the standard operation works.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Endophthalmitis** | The other superlative, and this chapter asks for it separately at `opmcq-c9-11` on the very next line of the page. Common it is not — it is the feared rarity |\n| **B. Posterior capsular opacification** | The key |\n| **C. Recurrence** | ⚠️ **The option worth thinking about, because it is impossible rather than merely uncommon.** The cataractous lens has been removed; there is no lens left to opacify again. PCO is the closest thing the eye can offer, and the name *after-cataract* is precisely the confusion this distractor trades on. A cataract does not recur |\n| **D. All of the above** | Fails on C alone |\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t9-365` keys the identical fact as a true statement — \"The most common is opacification of posterior capsule\" — inside a four-statement question whose other limbs test endophthalmitis treatment and phaco-versus-ECCE astigmatism.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Posterior capsule opacification is the commonest complication of cataract surgery: lens epithelial cells migrate across the capsule deliberately left to support the IOL. A cataract itself cannot recur — the lens is gone.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.64 (Ch.9 Q10; ⚠️ identical option list, in identical order, to Ch.9 Q11 — the stems differ only in "most common" vs "most serious")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most serious postoperative complication following treatment of cataract?",
    options:["Endophthalmitis","Posterior capsular opacification","Recurrence","All of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **THIS QUESTION AND `opmcq-c9-10` PRINT THE SAME FOUR OPTIONS IN THE SAME ORDER, ON THE SAME PAGE, AND KEY DIFFERENT LETTERS.** The only difference is one word of the stem — *most **serious*** here, *most **common*** there. The full timing-and-ranking table is written once, at `opmcq-c9-10`; this entry explains only what makes endophthalmitis the serious one.\n\n**`L18) Cataract II` slide 16 gives it the title in its opening line — \"Most serious vision-threatening complication\" — and then explains why in three lines.**\n\n> **Timing:** 24–72 hrs (acute) or weeks (delayed)\n> **Causative agents:** CoNS (**Staphylococcus epidermidis** most common), S. aureus, streptococci\n> **Signs:** pain, redness, reduced VA, **hypopyon**, yellowish vitreous haze\n> **Tx: urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime)**. Severe: vitrectomy\n\nThree features make it the emergency of this chapter. It is **intraocular infection**, so the tissue at risk is retina rather than a membrane. It is **fast**, arriving inside 72 hours while the patient is still expecting to improve. And its treatment is **intravitreal**, not topical — the deck's own flash-card slide repeats the drugs verbatim: \"Q: Most serious post-op complication? A: Endophthalmitis — intravitreal vancomycin + ceftazidime.\" The deck's *Dropless Cataract Surgery* slide adds the prophylaxis that follows from all this: intracameral cefuroxime or moxifloxacin at the end of the case.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Endophthalmitis** | The key |\n| **B. Posterior capsular opacification** | The **common** one, not the serious one — `opmcq-c9-10`. It is also entirely reversible in an outpatient clinic with a YAG laser (`opmcq-c9-7`), which is close to the opposite of serious |\n| **C. Recurrence** | Impossible — the lens has been removed. See `opmcq-c9-10` |\n| **D. All of the above** | Fails |\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t9-391` asks for \"the worst complication that may happen after cataract surgery\" and keys **endophthalmitis** against corneal oedema, iris prolapse and raised ocular tension — a menu of three genuine but survivable complications, where House offers a menu built from its own twin question. `opqb-t9-355` runs it as a case: 48 hours post-extraction, pain, visual loss, ciliary injection, corneal oedema and absent red reflex, keyed **bacterial endophthalmitis** as the first suspicion.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Endophthalmitis is the most serious complication of cataract surgery — intraocular infection within 24–72 hours, hypopyon and vitreous haze, treated by urgent vitreous tap and intravitreal vancomycin plus ceftazidime.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.64 (Ch.9 Q11; ⚠️ identical option list, in identical order, to Ch.9 Q10 — the stems differ only in "most serious" vs "most common")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"Which of the following causes jet black appearance of the pupillary aperture?",
    options:["Cataract","Pseudophakia","Posterior lens dislocation","All of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book answers this in one sentence, with the keyed option inside its parentheses.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.10, under *Pupil*:\n\n> The colour of the pupil is jet black in the newly born and starts to acquire a greyish tinge as the person gets older due to the continuous addition of new lens fibres throughout life causing light scattering; a process known as **senile sclerosis**.\n>\n> **In the absence of the lens** (surgically removed or **posteriorly dislocated**) the pupil regains its **jet-black colour**.\n\n**The pupil is a hole, and its colour is whatever the eye behind it does with light.** A clear young lens returns almost nothing to the observer, so the aperture reads black. Every added lens fibre scatters a little more light forward, which is the grey of age. An opaque lens scatters most of it, which is white. Remove the lens from the pupillary axis altogether — by surgery, or by letting it fall backwards into the vitreous — and the scattering source is gone, so the black returns.\n\n| Option | What the pupil actually looks like |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Cataract** | The opposite end of the same scale. `L17) Cataract I` lists among the signs \"**Pupil colour change (grey, white, brown)**\" and, in children, \"**leukocoria** ('white pupil')\"; `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 20 captions its three pupil photographs \"Black (Newly born & Aphakia)\" · \"Grayish in old age\" · \"**Leukocoria (Mature cataract)**\" |\n| **B. Pseudophakia** | ⚠️ **The good distractor, because the natural lens is gone here too — and the pupil is still not black.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 18 gives pseudophakia \"IOL visible in pupillary area; haptics may be seen at periphery; **Purkinje reflexes from IOL surfaces**\" and \"clear **red reflex** if no PCO\". An implanted lens has its own reflecting surfaces; the aperture is not empty |\n| **C. Posterior lens dislocation** | The key, and the book's own parenthesis |\n| **D. All of the above** | Fails on A and B |\n\n**The same appearance from the front is what `opmcq-c9-17` describes from the side.** `L18` slide 18 groups the aphakic signs together: \"**Iridodonesis** (tremulous iris); **deep anterior chamber**; **jet-black (dark) pupil**; absence of lens\" — one missing lens, four findings. `L3,4` slide 26 supplies the anterior chamber half of it, tabulating **Aphakia** under *Deep AC* alongside high myopia and buphthalmos.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t9-358` asks what characterises posterior dislocation of the lens and keys a combination that includes uveitis as a complication; its first option is a garbled printing of the loss of a Purkinje image — the same optical fact as this question, since a dislocated lens takes its reflecting surfaces out of the pupil with it. `L17` uses that sign for the opposite purpose: \"Lens opacity (**absent 3rd Purkinje image**, slit lamp)\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A jet-black pupil means nothing in the pupillary axis is scattering light — the lens has gone, surgically or by posterior dislocation. Cataract whitens the pupil and an IOL returns Purkinje reflexes and a red reflex.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.64 (Ch.9 Q12)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"Which of the following is true regarding the crystalline lens?",
    options:["Anterior lens surface is flatter than posterior surface","The lens lacks nerves, vessels or connective tissue","The lens is a semi-transparent biconvex structure","A & B","A & C"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ *Verified at 400 dpi: five options, letters A–E in order, key D.*\n\n**Two of the three statements are printed almost word for word in the anatomy slide; the third fails on a single prefix.**\n\n| Statement | The course's wording | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Anterior surface is flatter than posterior** | `L18) Cataract II` slide 3: \"Shape: Biconvex — **steeper posterior curve**\". A steeper back means a flatter front | **TRUE** |\n| **B. The lens lacks nerves, vessels or connective tissue** | `L18` slide 3: \"Structure: **Avascular**, transparent — **nutrition via aqueous**\". `L17) Cataract I` gives the entire composition as three items — \"Outer **capsule** (basement membrane like material) · Lens **epithelial cells** (lining the anterior capsule) · Lens **fibres**\" — and there is no fourth | **TRUE** |\n| **C. The lens is a semi-transparent biconvex structure** | Biconvex is right and *semi*-transparent is wrong. `L18` slide 3 says \"Avascular, **transparent**\" and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.10 says \"the elastic, **transparent**, biconvex structure located directly behind the iris\". Neither hedges | **FALSE** |\n\n**Why avascularity matters rather than being a fact to memorise.** A tissue with no blood supply is fed by diffusion from the aqueous, and a tissue with no nerves cannot report that it is in trouble — which is the anatomical reason cataract is painless. `L17`'s symptom heading is \"**Gradual progressive painless** diminution of vision\", and that painlessness is written into slide 3. Transparency is the same argument: any vessel or nerve inside the lens would scatter light, so the lens can only stay clear by having none.\n\n**The two facts the stem leaves out, both worth carrying.** `L17`: \"Equatorial diameter ~ **9 mm**, anteroposterior diameter ~ **4 mm**\", and \"Responsible for **⅓ of the refractive power of the eye**\" — `L18` puts a number on it, \"~**18–20 D** (one-third of total ~60 D)\", with \"Refractive index: **1.386 (nucleus) — increases with age**\", the line `opmcq-c9-4` turns into a symptom.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A / B / C** | Individually true, true, false as tabulated — and none is offered alone as the key |\n| **D. A & B** | The key |\n| **E. A & C** | ⚠️ The trap, and it is a good one: it pairs the true statement with the one that is 90 % true. A reader who checks *biconvex* and stops will take it |\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain asks the optics rather than the histology. `opqb-t9-366` keys the dioptric power of the lens **outside** the eye as 70 D — the lens is far more powerful in air than the ~18–20 D it contributes in situ, because in the eye it is surrounded by aqueous and vitreous of nearly its own refractive index.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The crystalline lens is biconvex with a steeper posterior curve, and it is avascular, non-innervated and fully transparent — which is why cataract is painless and why the option saying 'semi-transparent' is the false one.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.64 (Ch.9 Q13)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"When lens capsule becomes intumescent, it results in which of the following types of conditions?",
    options:["Phacomorphic glaucoma","Phacolytic glaucoma","Morgagnian cataract","Cataracta nigra","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ *The option list is split across the 64|65 seam — stem and options A, B at the foot of p.64, options C, D, E and the key at the head of p.65. Both ends read. ⚠️ Note also that it is the **lens** that becomes intumescent, not the capsule; the course never applies the word to the capsule.*\n\n**`L17) Cataract I` prints the whole causal chain, and \"intumescence\" is its first link.**\n\n> **Intumescence** (lens fibres liquefaction and fluid imbibition → **swelling of lens**)\n>\n> Lens induced glaucoma → **Phacomorphic** → Intumescence (lens fibres fluid imbibition) → **increased lens thickness → pupil block → elevated IOP**\n\n*Morph* is shape. A lens that imbibes fluid gets thicker, a thicker lens pushes the iris forward against it, aqueous can no longer pass through the pupil, and the peripheral iris is bowed into the angle — pupil block closing the angle. `L18) Cataract II` slide 26 tabulates the result: \"Phacomorphic Glaucoma | **Immature cataract** | **Swollen lens → angle closure** | Red eye + pain + HM | Raised IOP, corneal oedema | **Urgent phaco**\", and its flash-card slide states the pair in one line — \"Phacomorphic: **swollen immature lens → angle closure**. Phacolytic: mature → protein leaks → macrophage blockage of TM.\" `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 26 tabulates **intumescent cataract** among the causes of a **shallow anterior chamber**, which is the same event seen at the slit lamp.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Phacomorphic glaucoma** | The key |\n| **B. Phacolytic glaucoma** | ⚠️ **The other lens-induced glaucoma, and it is the mirror image at every point** — a *later* stage (hypermature, not swollen), a *chemical* rather than mechanical mechanism, and an *open* angle. `opmcq-c9-15` is that question, printed next and using this identical option list |\n| **C. Morgagnian cataract** | A hypermature cataract, not a glaucoma. `L17`: \"Morgagnian (liquefaction of cortex and **sinking of nucleus**)\". Defined at `opmcq-c9-19` |\n| **D. Cataracta nigra** | A black cataract — the far end of `L17`'s nuclear colour sequence \"grey, yellow brown, **black**\". A dense hard nucleus, not a swollen one. ⚠️ Measured: **`nigra` returns exactly zero hits, case-insensitively, across all 29 cached decks and all 12 cached book chapters** — the entity is taught, the Latin name is not. *(The name itself is **not taken from the course material**.)* |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n**Both lens-induced glaucomas are surgical emergencies, and that is the practical point.** `L18` slide 15 lists \"Phacomorphic glaucoma — swollen lens causing angle closure\" and \"Phacolytic glaucoma\" side by side under **URGENT / MANDATORY** indications for surgery — the treatment is to take the lens out, not to treat the pressure. Grade Gain keys the same at `opqb-t9-351`: phacolytic glaucoma is best treated by **cataract extraction**, ahead of a fistulising operation, a cyclodestructive procedure or drops.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t9-404` runs the phacomorphic story as a case — months of mild blurring, then sudden pain, red eye and a stony-hard eye — and keys \"**swollen intumescent lens**\" as the slit-lamp finding. `opqb-t9-352` asks which lens condition is *least* likely to cause lens-induced glaucoma and rules out **posterior** dislocation, since a lens that has fallen out of the pupil can neither block it nor leak into the angle in the same way.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"An intumescent lens is a swollen lens: increased thickness causes pupil block and angle closure — phacomorphic glaucoma, an urgent indication for lens extraction.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.64-65 (Ch.9 Q14; the option list is split across the page break — stem and options A, B on p.64, options C, D, E and the key on p.65. ⚠️ Identical option list, in identical order, to Ch.9 Q15)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"When lens capsule becomes wrinkled, it results in which of the following types of conditions?",
    options:["Phacomorphic glaucoma","Phacolytic glaucoma","Morgagnian cataract","Cataracta nigra","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **THIS QUESTION AND `opmcq-c9-14` PRINT THE SAME FIVE OPTIONS IN THE SAME ORDER AND KEY DIFFERENT LETTERS.** The only difference is the adjective — *wrinkled* here, *intumescent* there. The chain for phacomorphic glaucoma is written once, at `opmcq-c9-14`.\n\n**A wrinkled capsule is a specific stage with a name, and `L18) Cataract II` slide 7 attaches the glaucoma to it in the same cell.** Under Hypermature:\n\n> **SHRUNKEN type: cortex leaks → wrinkled capsule.** MORGAGNIAN type: liquefied cortex, nucleus sinks inferiorly (pathognomonic pseudohypopyon-like appearance). **Risk: phacolytic glaucoma**\n\n`L17) Cataract I` gives the same stage its full definition — \"Hypermature (**shrinkage of lens capsule**, liquefaction of cortex)\", subdivided into \"**Typical** (shrinkage of and calcification and cholesterol deposits on lens capsule)\" and \"Morgagnian\" — and then prints the mechanism in two steps:\n\n> **Phacolytic** → **Degeneration of lens capsule → leakage of lens proteins** → immune reaction in anterior chamber (uveitis) → uveitic glaucoma · **clogging of TM by lens protein macromolecules → secondary open angle glaucoma (trabecular)**\n\n**The wrinkle is the evidence, not the cause.** A hypermature lens has liquefied its cortex and let the liquid out through a degenerate capsule; losing volume, the capsule shrinks and wrinkles. The same leak carries lens protein into the anterior chamber, where macrophages engorge with it and block the trabecular meshwork. So the visible wrinkle and the raised pressure are two readings of one event — *lyt* is dissolution, as *morph* is shape.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Phacomorphic glaucoma** | The *swollen* lens, an earlier stage and a closed angle — `opmcq-c9-14` |\n| **B. Phacolytic glaucoma** | The key |\n| **C. Morgagnian cataract** | ⚠️ **The most interesting distractor, because it is the same stage by a different route.** Both are hypermature, and `L18` slide 7 puts them in one cell — but the shrunken type wrinkles its capsule while the Morgagnian type keeps its volume and lets the nucleus sink through the liquefied cortex. The stem says *wrinkled*, which is the shrunken type. Note also that Morgagnian is a **cataract**, and the question asks what condition **results** |\n| **D. Cataracta nigra** | A black hard nucleus, unrelated to capsular change. See `opmcq-c9-14` |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t9-405` keys \"**hyper mature cortical cataract**\" as the stage in which phacolytic glaucoma occurs, and `opqb-t9-383` asks what complication a Morgagnian cataract can cause, keying **acute rise of IOP** — which is this same glaucoma, arrived at from the other hypermature type. `opqb-t9-351` keys **cataract extraction** as its treatment.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A wrinkled, shrunken capsule is the hypermature cataract whose liquefied cortex has leaked; the escaped lens protein and its macrophages block the trabecular meshwork — phacolytic glaucoma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.65 (Ch.9 Q15; ⚠️ identical option list, in identical order, to Ch.9 Q14 — the stems differ only in "wrinkled" vs "intumescent")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"Which of the following is best described as (presence of IOL inside the eye that was previously surgically implanted)?",
    options:["Aphakia","Pseudophakia","Ectopia lentis","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ *The parentheses in the stem are the bank's own.*\n\n**`L18) Cataract II` slide 18 is titled for exactly this pair and defines both in its two columns.**\n\n| | **PSEUDOPHAKIA — Eye with Artificial IOL** | **APHAKIA — No Lens** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cause | Cataract surgery with implantation | ICCE (historical); posterior IOL dislocation into vitreous; traumatic lens loss |\n| Slit lamp | IOL visible in the pupillary area; haptics may be seen at the periphery; **Purkinje reflexes from IOL surfaces** | **Jet-black pupil**, **deep anterior chamber**, **iridodonesis**, absence of lens |\n| Refraction | Usually emmetropic if biometry correct; **no accommodation** | **+10 to +12 D hypermetropia** in a previously emmetropic eye |\n| Posterior capsule | Remains, supports the IOL in the bag; can opacify → YAG | — |\n| Correction | — | Aphakic spectacles (+10–12 D: thick, minifying, **ring scotoma**); contact lens; secondary IOL |\n\n*Phakos* is the lens; *pseudo-* is false and *a-* is without. The word therefore encodes the stem exactly: a false lens is present.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Aphakia** | The literal opposite — nothing in the pupillary axis at all, whether from an intracapsular extraction, a dislocated implant or trauma. Its optical penalty is severe: `L17) Cataract I` measures the image magnification of aphakic **glasses** at ~**30 %** (\"monocular aphakia → least suitable → anisokonia → binocular diplopia\"), a **contact lens** at ~7–10 %, and an **IOL** at ~**3 %** — \"most physiological\" |\n| **B. Pseudophakia** | The key |\n| **C. Ectopia lentis** | A lens that is present but out of place. ⚠️ Measured: **`ectopia` returns exactly zero hits, case-insensitively, across all 29 cached decks and all 12 cached book chapters** — the *term* is not taught, though the condition is, as **subluxation** and **dislocation** (`L25) Trauma I` slide 34 \"Subluxation — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)\"; `L18` slide 15 \"Subluxated lens with acute pupillary block\"; `book/ch16-paediatric` \"Subluxated lens (rare) due to stretch of the zonular fibers\" in buphthalmos). This chapter uses the term again at `opmcq-c9-23`. *(That ectopia lentis is the umbrella name for subluxation and dislocation is **not taken from the course material**.)* |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n**The one clinical consequence of pseudophakia the deck flags.** The IOL does not accommodate — slide 18 calls it a \"presbyopia-like state\" — which is why premium optics exist at all: slide 14 offers monofocal, multifocal (\"halos/glare tradeoff\"), EDOF and toric IOLs to buy back some of the range a natural lens gave for free.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain does not ask the definition; it asks where the implant goes. `opqb-t9-354` keys the **capsular bag** as the best site, matching slide 14's \"In the capsular bag (preferred) or ciliary sulcus. Most physiological position\", and `opqb-t9-367` keys an **anterior chamber IOL after intracapsular extraction** — the no-capsule case.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Pseudophakia is an eye carrying a surgically implanted IOL; aphakia is an eye with no lens at all, +10 to +12 D hypermetropic with a deep chamber, tremulous iris and jet-black pupil.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.65 (Ch.9 Q16; the parentheses are the bank\'s)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"Which of the following is best described as (tremulousness of the iris with eye movement)?",
    options:["Iridodialysis","Iridodonesis","Iridocyclitis","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ *The parentheses in the stem are the bank's own.*\n\n**The course prints the term and its gloss together, twice, and the phrasing is almost the stem's.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 18, among the classic signs of **aphakia**: \"**Iridodonesis (tremulous iris)**; deep anterior chamber; jet-black (dark) pupil; absence of lens\". `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 5 lists \"**Tremulous iris (iridodonesis)**\" among the iris diseases, and `book/ch10-uveal-tract` reprints the same list.\n\n**Why a missing lens makes the iris shake.** The iris normally rests against the anterior lens surface, which supports it. Remove the lens — or loosen its zonules so it drops back — and the iris is left as an unsupported curtain in aqueous; every saccade sets it wobbling for a moment afterwards. That is why the sign travels with the other two aphakic findings on the same slide: nothing behind the iris means it can also fall back, so the anterior chamber deepens (`L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 26 tabulates **aphakia** under *deep AC*), and nothing in the pupil means the pupil goes black (`opmcq-c9-12`). *(The mechanical explanation is **not taken from the course material**; both decks print the term as a labelled heading without explaining it, and `book/ch10-uveal-tract` records the gap explicitly — the iris headings on that page are \"LISTED and PICTURED but NOT DEFINED anywhere in the chapter\".)*\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Iridodialysis** | ⚠️ **Printed on the same slide as the key**, which is what makes it the intended trap: `L13,14` slide 5 branches an \"Iris defect\" box into iridectomy, **iridodialysis**, coloboma and aniridia. *-dialysis* is a separation — the iris root torn away from the ciliary body, classically by blunt trauma, giving a second crescentic pupil at the periphery. A structural defect that is visible at rest, not a movement |\n| **B. Iridodonesis** | The key. *-donesis*, from Greek *doneo*, to shake |\n| **C. Iridocyclitis** | Inflammation of iris and ciliary body — anterior uveitis. A different suffix and a different disease, and the subject of the very next chapter: the bank's own ch.10 Q2 keys it for a patient with keratic precipitates, hypopyon and a violaceous ciliary injection |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n**Hold the three by their suffixes.** *-donesis* = shaking · *-dialysis* = tearing apart · *-cyclitis* = inflammation of the ciliary body. Only the first is a sign of a lens that is absent or loose.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Iridodonesis is a tremulous iris — an iris left unsupported by an absent or subluxated lens, travelling with a deep anterior chamber and a jet-black pupil; iridodialysis is a torn iris root and iridocyclitis is anterior uveitis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.65 (Ch.9 Q17; the parentheses are the bank\'s)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"Which of the following is NOT among the indications of cataract surgery?",
    options:["Secondary acute elevation of the IOP as in phacomorphic / phacolytic glaucoma","Congenital cataract","Visually significant traumatic cataract","Impaired vision","None of the above"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**Key E — the bank's position is that all four ARE indications, and `L18) Cataract II` slide 15 prints all four, split across its two columns.**\n\n| The bank's option | Where slide 15 puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **A.** Secondary acute IOP rise as in phacomorphic / phacolytic glaucoma | **URGENT / MANDATORY** — \"Phacomorphic glaucoma — swollen lens causing angle closure\" and \"Phacolytic glaucoma — leaking lens proteins → macrophage-induced raised IOP\", the top two entries in that column |\n| **B.** Congenital cataract | **URGENT / MANDATORY** — \"**Dense** congenital cataract — prevent amblyopia (**within weeks**)\" |\n| **C.** Visually significant traumatic cataract | The functional criterion applied to a traumatic lens: \"Visual impairment affecting quality of life\", \"Patient-reported functional disability despite glasses update\" |\n| **D.** Impaired vision | The elective headline, and the commonest indication of all |\n\n**`L17) Cataract I` reduces the whole slide to two determinants, and they are exactly the two the options divide along.** Its treatment triangle names them — **functional significance of the opacity** and **complications** — and assigns:\n\n> **Observation** (functionally insignificant + no complications) · **Refractive correction** (functionally significant, amenable to improvement by refraction, no complications) · **Surgery** (**functionally significant and / or complications**)\n\nOption A is a complication. Option D is functional significance. Options B and C are both. Nothing on the menu falls outside the triangle's surgical arm, which is why the answer is E.\n\n⚠️ **One nuance the printed option flattens, recorded rather than disputed.** The deck's urgent entry is \"**dense** congenital cataract\", not congenital cataract as such, and a small non-axial congenital opacity is watched rather than operated. Grade Gain keys precisely that at `opqb-t9-384`: a 6-month infant with a localised dot-shaped **anterior polar** cataract and no other abnormality is best managed by **follow-up with visual acuity testing**. House's option B is unqualified and the key requires it to be read as the dense kind. The urgency, when it applies, is the deck's own: *within weeks*, because a dense congenital cataract deprives the developing visual system and the resulting amblyopia is not reversible later.\n\n⚠️ **The other thing worth noticing is what is absent from the menu.** `L18` slide 15 also lists \"VA below driving standard (typically **<6/12** in the driving eye)\", \"**monocular diplopia** interfering with daily activities\", \"to facilitate management of posterior segment disease (e.g. diabetic retinopathy requiring laser/OCT monitoring)\" and \"refractive cataract surgery\". The last is the modern one: an eye may be operated for its refraction rather than for its opacity.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain tests the negative case instead. `opqb-t9-374` withholds surgery from an eye whose 6/60 is amblyopic rather than cataractous, and `opqb-t9-409` keys **bad light projection** as the marker of a poor prognosis — the counterpart of `L18` slide 5's \"Projection of light: **good in all 4 directions = good prognosis**\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Cataract surgery is indicated whenever the opacity is functionally significant or has caused a complication: impaired vision, a visually significant traumatic cataract, a dense congenital cataract within weeks, and urgently for phacomorphic or phacolytic glaucoma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.65 (Ch.9 Q18, the last question on the page — about a third of p.65 below its answer rule is blank)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"Which of the following best describes (Morgagnian cataract)?",
    options:["It's a form of mature cataract with lens cortex liquefaction causing the dark nucleus to sink inferiorly inside the lens capsule","It's a form of hypermature cataract with lens cortex liquefaction causing the dark nucleus to sink inferiorly inside the lens capsule","It's a type of cataract in which the lens nucleus acquires a dark brown to black color","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ *The parentheses in the stem are the bank's own. Options A and B are **identical except for the single word mature / hypermature** — that word is the entire question.*\n\n**Both decks file the Morgagnian cataract under HYPERMATURE, and neither ever calls it mature.** `L17) Cataract I`, under the cortical stages:\n\n> **Hypermature** (shrinkage of lens capsule, liquefaction of cortex)\n>  · **Typical** (shrinkage of and calcification and cholesterol deposits on lens capsule)\n>  · **Morgagnian** (liquefaction of cortex and **sinking of nucleus**)\n\n`L18) Cataract II` slide 7 puts the two subtypes in the same hypermature cell: \"SHRUNKEN type: cortex leaks → wrinkled capsule. **MORGAGNIAN type: liquefied cortex, nucleus sinks inferiorly** (pathognomonic pseudohypopyon-like appearance). **Risk: phacolytic glaucoma.**\" `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 21 photographs the ladder in order — \"IMSC | MSC | **Hypermature shrunken type** | **Morgagnian cataract**\" — with Morgagnian at the end, past mature.\n\n**Why the distinction is not pedantry.** *Mature* means the cortex is completely opaque but still there (`L35,36) Chronic visual loss`: \"A **mature** cataract has a totally opacified cortex\"). *Hypermature* means the cortex has broken down into liquid. Liquefaction is the precondition for a nucleus to sink at all — a solid opaque cortex holds it in the centre. So the stage and the sign are not two facts but one: option A describes something that cannot physically happen.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Mature, cortex liquefied, nucleus sinks** | The trap — right sign, wrong stage, and the stage is what makes the sign possible |\n| **B. Hypermature, cortex liquefied, nucleus sinks** | The key |\n| **C. Nucleus acquires a dark brown to black colour** | **Cataracta nigra**, the far end of `L17`'s nuclear colour sequence \"Nuclear → grey, yellow brown, **black**\". A hard dark nucleus, not a dissolved cortex — and it is printed as option D of `opmcq-c9-14` and `opmcq-c9-15` |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n**Two consequences worth attaching to the name.** The liquefied cortex leaks lens protein, so the same eye is the phacolytic-glaucoma eye of `opmcq-c9-15` — Grade Gain asks it directly at `opqb-t9-383` and keys **acute rise of IOP**. And the zonules of such a lens are usually weak: `L18` slide 13 keeps one live indication for the otherwise historical intracapsular extraction — \"Still performed in **Morgagnian cataract with zonular weakness** (careful selection)\" — which is the one place `opmcq-c9-8`'s discarded technique is still the right operation.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A Morgagnian cataract is HYPERmature: the cortex has liquefied and the dark nucleus sinks inferiorly inside the capsule — the stage is what makes the sign possible, and it carries a risk of phacolytic glaucoma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.66 (Ch.9 Q19; the parentheses are the bank\'s. Options A and B differ only in the word "mature" vs "hypermature")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"A 58-year-old male patient presents with complete lens opacification. What is the best tool used to visualize retina in that patient?",
    options:["Ultrasonography","Ophthalmoscopy","Pachymetry","Pentacam"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**A completely opaque lens defines the problem: no optical instrument can see through it, so the retina has to be reached by sound instead of light.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 12 \"Pre-Operative Assessment\", under *Posterior Segment Evaluation*:\n\n> **B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible → rule out retinal detachment / tumour** · Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected · OCT when possible in hypermature cataract\n\nand slide 20's fundal-examination step ends \"If not visible → **B-scan ultrasound mandatory**\". `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` states the problem the scan solves — \"If the lens is densely cataractous, **retinal and optic nerve pathology cannot be adequately evaluated**\" — and lists **ultrasonography** in its own examination sequence.\n\n**What the answer buys is a decision, not a picture.** The eye behind a mature cataract may harbour a detachment or a tumour, and finding one changes whether the operation is worth doing at all. `L18` slide 5's low-technology substitutes work in the same direction — \"Projection of light: good in all 4 directions = good prognosis\" and \"Colour discrimination: red vs green test = good retinal function\" — but they test function, not anatomy. Ultrasound is the only tool on this menu that returns anatomy.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Ultrasonography** | The key |\n| **B. Ophthalmoscopy** | ⚠️ **The option the stem has already ruled out.** Ophthalmoscopy is exactly what fails when the media are opaque — that is the whole reason the question exists. `opmcq-c9-6` is the same eye described from the ophthalmoscope's side: absent red reflex |\n| **C. Pachymetry** | Corneal **thickness** measurement, which says nothing about the retina and nothing about the lens. `L15,16) Cornea` uses it for two purposes only — \"Pachymetry – minimum thickness\" before refractive surgery, and grading ectasia |\n| **D. Pentacam** | Corneal tomography — anterior and posterior corneal elevation plus a pachymetry map. It stops at the cornea. ⚠️ **The bank is reusing its own answer as a distractor**: `opmcq-c8-6` keys **Pentacam** as the decision tool for a child with keratoconus. Right instrument, wrong tissue, one chapter later. ⚠️ Measured: **`pentacam` returns exactly zero hits, case-insensitively, across all 29 cached decks and all 12 cached book chapters** — the instrument is not named anywhere in this course. *(**Not taken from the course material.**)* |\n\n**Cross-bank, and Grade Gain runs this question four ways.** `opqb-t9-371` asks how to check for **retinal detachment** behind a mature cataract and keys **US** over direct and indirect ophthalmoscopy. `opqb-t9-376` gives a diabetic with bilateral mature cataracts, fundus not seen, and keys **ultrasonography** as the test of value before surgery. `opqb-t9-379` supplies the finding that would contraindicate operating the worse eye — **massive tractional retinal detachment**, discovered by that scan. And `opqb-t9-378` shows the cost of not looking: after extraction the vision reaches only 6/36, because of a macular lesion no pre-operative test had caught.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"When the lens is completely opaque, B-scan ultrasound is the only way to assess the retina — it rules out detachment or tumour before surgery; ophthalmoscopy needs clear media, and pachymetry and Scheimpflug tomography image the cornea.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.66 (Ch.9 Q20)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"Which of the following is the innermost layer of the crystalline lens?",
    options:["Fetal nucleus","Embryonic nucleus","Adult nucleus","Lens cortex"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The zonation is taught, and it is taught in a figure rather than in prose — which is why a text search for it nearly came back empty.** `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 17 is captioned *zones of the lens* and carries a sectional drawing with the labels bracketed down the left:\n\n> Anterior capsule · Anterior epithelium · **Cortex** · **Nucleus** — bracketed into four zones, listed top to bottom: **Adult · Infantile · Fetal · Embryonic**\n\nOutermost to innermost, in that order. The embryonic nucleus is the last one named and the deepest.\n\n**The order is not arbitrary — it is a growth record, and the mechanism is printed in two other decks.** `L17) Cataract I` gives the composition as \"Lens fibres (form by **continuous division of lens epithelial cells at lens equator** then elongation)\", and `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` spells out the consequence: \"The normal lens **continues to grow throughout life**. The epithelial cells continue to produce new cortical lens fibers, yielding a slow increase in size, weight, and density over the years.\" New fibres are laid down at the surface and press the older ones inwards, so the lens is built like a tree in rings — and the oldest fibres, the ones present before birth, end up at the very centre. The innermost zone is therefore the earliest one by definition.\n\n| Option | Where it sits |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Fetal nucleus** | ⚠️ **The near miss — second innermost.** It is the shell immediately *around* the embryonic nucleus, laid down later in gestation. A reader who remembers only that the deep zones are prenatal will take it |\n| **B. Embryonic nucleus** | The key — innermost, formed first |\n| **C. Adult nucleus** | The outermost of the four nuclear zones, added continuously after maturity. It is the zone whose densification produces `opmcq-c9-2`'s yellow nucleus and `opmcq-c9-4`'s myopic shift |\n| **D. Lens cortex** | Not part of the nucleus at all, and outside all four zones. `L18) Cataract II` slide 3's cross-section runs **Capsule → Anterior epithelium → Cortex → Nucleus** from outside in, so the cortex is the outermost of everything offered |\n\n**Two clinical hooks for the same anatomy.** Congenital cataracts are named for the zone they occupy — `L17` lists \"**lamellar**, blue dot, anterior pyramidal, posterior polar, membranous, sutural\", and a lamellar cataract is an opaque shell at one growth ring, which is why the eye can still see past it. And the layered structure is the reason the deck's morphological classification (`opmcq-c9-2`) is possible at all: nuclear, cortical and posterior subcapsular are three depths in one organ.\n\n⚠️ **A correction worth recording.** A case-insensitive grep for `embryonic` returns hits in only two cached files and `fetal nucleus` returns none, so this question looked at first like a gap to be filled from outside knowledge. It is not — `L1,2` slide 17 teaches it in full. The absence was manufactured by searching for prose in a deck that draws.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The lens nucleus is zoned embryonic, fetal, infantile, adult from the centre outward, because fibres are added at the equator throughout life and press the oldest ones inward — the embryonic nucleus is innermost.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.66 (Ch.9 Q21)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding senile cataract except?",
    options:["It occurs in patients above 40 years of age","It comprises 90% of cataract cases","It's either incipient, immature, mature or hypermature","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **THIS QUESTION AND `opmcq-c9-26` PRINT THE SAME THREE STATEMENTS, IN THE SAME ORDER, ONE PAGE APART.** Only the fourth option and the polarity of the stem differ — *except* with \"None of the above\" here, no *except* with \"All of the above\" there. Both key **D**, so the two are consistent: the bank's position is that all three statements are true. The statements are worked through once, here.\n\n| Statement | The course's position | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Occurs above 40 years of age** | `L17) Cataract I` twice: morphologically \"Senile (**starting from the 5th decade**)\", and in the epidemiology block \"Senile cataract **starts from the 5th decade onwards**, with no gender or laterality predilection\". The fifth decade of life is ages 40–49 | **TRUE** |\n| **B. Comprises 90% of cataract cases** | ⚠️ **The figure is not in this course.** Measured: `90%` returns five cached files — `L1,2`, `L15,16) Cornea`, `L24) Ocular tumors.`, `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` and `book/ch06-conjunctiva` — and the only lens-adjacent hit, in `L35,36`, is about **dry age-related macular degeneration**, not cataract. What the course does say is the ranking without a number: `L18) Cataract II` slide 4 heads the senile column \"**Most common** — age-related\", `L17` calls senile cataract \"the most common cause of reversible blindness in the world\", and `L35,36` gives the only quantities it has — cataract in \"approximately **22.9%** of elderly population (aged above 60) in Egypt\", roughly 2.2 million people. That age-related cataract is around nine in ten of all cataract in adult practice is the conventional figure and is **not taken from the course material** | **TRUE (outside the course)** |\n| **C. Either incipient, immature, mature or hypermature** | `L18` slide 7 \"Classification by Maturity\" is exactly those four rows in that order, and `L17` lists them under Cortical (stages): \"Incipient → water vacuoles · Immature → incomplete opacification · Mature → complete opacification · Hypermature (shrinkage of lens capsule, liquefaction of cortex)\" | **TRUE** |\n\n⚠️ **A DECK-vs-DECK DIVERGENCE ON STATEMENT C, RECORDED AND NOT RESOLVED.** `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` prints its own maturity list as \"**immature, mature, itumescent, hypermature**\" [sic] — substituting **intumescent** for **incipient**, and dropping the earliest stage. `L17` and `L18` agree with each other and with the bank against it. The key is unaffected, since the bank's list is the one the two cataract lectures print; the divergence is recorded here because a reader working from `L35,36` alone would mark statement C false.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A / B / C** | All true, so none is the exception |\n| **D. None of the above** | The key — there is no false statement |\n\n**Why the age threshold is worth more than it looks.** \"Above 40\" is not a description of old age; it is the reason `opmcq-c9-3`'s 52-year-old is investigated rather than congratulated, and the reason `opqb-t9-398`'s 45-year-old driver has a nuclear cataract at all.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Senile cataract begins in the fifth decade, is the great majority of adult cataract, and passes through incipient, immature, mature and hypermature stages — all three of the bank's statements are true.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.66 (Ch.9 Q22; ⚠️ options A, B and C are identical, in identical order, to Ch.9 Q26 on p.67 — only option D and the word "except" differ, and both questions key D)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"Ectopia lentis can be caused by which of the following conditions?",
    options:["Marfan syndrome","Homocystinuria","Trauma","All of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The lens hangs from its zonules, so anything that weakens or tears them displaces it. All three options do.** `L17) Cataract I` states the suspension — \"suspended in place by the **zonular fibres** (attached to the ciliary processes)\" — and `L18) Cataract II` slide 3 repeats it: \"Zonules from ciliary processes → equatorial capsule\".\n\n**The two syndromes are taught with their directions, and the directions are the teaching point.** `L18`'s rapid-fire revision slide:\n\n> Q: **Marfan** lens direction? A: **Superior-temporal** subluxation (**zonules intact inferiorly**)\n>\n> Q: **Homocystinuria** lens direction? A: **Inferior-nasal** (**all zonules weak, lens falls down**)\n\nThe mechanism is inside each answer. In Marfan the fibrillin defect weakens the zonules **unevenly**, so the intact inferior fibres pull the lens up and out. In homocystinuria the whole zonular apparatus is weak, so nothing holds the lens and gravity takes it down and in. Same displacement, opposite vectors, and the vector names the disease.\n\n| Option | The course's evidence |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Marfan syndrome** | `L18`'s flash card above. ⚠️ Note that `Marfan` also appears twice elsewhere in this module for a different sign entirely — **blue sclera** (`book/ch07-sclera`: thin sclera with increased visibility of underlying choroidal pigment, \"Marfan syndrome, Osteogenesis imperfecta\"; `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` records it as an absence in its own deck). The lens teaching is `L18`'s alone |\n| **B. Homocystinuria** | `L18`'s flash card above. Measured: **`homocystin` returns exactly one cached file — `L18) Cataract II`** — so that slide is the module's only teaching on it |\n| **C. Trauma** | `L25) Trauma I` slide 34 is titled \"Blunt trauma: **Subluxation**\" and captions it \"**Subluxation — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)**\", giving the symptom a displaced lens produces: two images from one eye, because light passes both through the lens and around its edge. `L18` slide 15 lists \"**Subluxated lens** with acute pupillary block\" among the urgent indications for surgery, and slide 13 keeps ECCE for \"subluxated lenses\" |\n| **D. All of the above** | The key |\n\n⚠️ **THE TERM ITSELF IS NOT IN THIS COURSE, THOUGH THE CONDITION IS TAUGHT THREE TIMES.** Measured: **`ectopia` returns exactly zero hits, case-insensitively, across all 29 cached decks and all 12 cached book chapters.** The course calls it **subluxation** (partially displaced, still in the pupillary axis) and **dislocation** (out of it altogether — `opmcq-c9-12`'s posteriorly dislocated lens). *That ectopia lentis is the umbrella term for both is **not taken from the course material**.*\n\n**One more cause the menu omits, and the course does print it:** `book/ch16-paediatric` gives \"**Subluxated lens** (rare) due to **stretch of the zonular fibers**\" as a feature of **buphthalmos** — an eye enlarged by congenital glaucoma outgrows its own zonules. Mechanically it belongs with homocystinuria: a global zonular failure rather than a sectoral one.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Ectopia lentis follows any zonular failure: Marfan (superotemporal, inferior zonules intact), homocystinuria (inferonasal, all zonules weak), blunt trauma (subluxation with uniocular diplopia) — and buphthalmos by stretch.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.66 (Ch.9 Q23, the last question on the page)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"Which of the following medications can cause cataract?",
    options:["Beta blockers","NSAIDs","Phenothiazine","All of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The course prints a cataractogenic drug list, and a phenothiazine heads it — under its drug name rather than its class name.** `L35,36) Chronic visual loss`, in its cataract block:\n\n> **Cataratogenic drugs** [sic]\n> **Chlorpromazine** · Miotics · Myleran · Amiodarone · gold\n\n**Chlorpromazine is the prototype phenothiazine**, so option C is sourced after all — the class word is simply never written down. *(The identification of chlorpromazine as a phenothiazine is **not taken from the course material**: measured, **`phenothiazine` returns exactly zero hits, case-insensitively, across all 29 cached decks and all 12 cached book chapters**, while `chlorpromazine` returns exactly one — the list above.)* The mechanism is a dose- and duration-dependent deposition of pigment granules in the anterior lens capsule and epithelium in patients on long-term high-dose antipsychotics, classically with matching corneal deposits — **also not taken from the course material**, which prints the drug name and nothing else.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Beta blockers** | ⚠️ **The distractor most likely to be taken, because beta blockers are in the eye constantly** — they are the mainstay of medical glaucoma treatment in `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` and `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry`. They are **not** on the cataractogenic list. Note that the list's second entry, **miotics** (pilocarpine), *is* a glaucoma drop — so the trap is not \"glaucoma drugs cause cataract\" but \"this particular glaucoma drug does not\". Measured: `beta.block` returns no hit in any cataract context in any cached file |\n| **B. NSAIDs** | Not on the list either, and this chapter's own deck uses them for the opposite purpose: `L18) Cataract II` slide 16 treats postoperative **cystoid macular oedema** with \"topical NSAIDs + steroids\". A drug used to settle the eye after lens surgery |\n| **C. Phenothiazine** | The key |\n| **D. All of the above** | Fails on A and B |\n\n⚠️ **THE COURSE'S HEADLINE CATARACTOGENIC DRUG IS NOT ON THIS MENU AT ALL, AND IT IS THE ONE TO CARRY OUT OF THE CHAPTER: the corticosteroid.** `L17) Cataract I` lists \"**Topical steroids**\" among the local ocular causes of complicated cataract; `L18` slide 6 gives the PSC causes as \"**steroids**, DM, uveitis, irradiation, myotonic dystrophy\" and warns \"PSC + young patient + systemic steroids = **Always ask about steroid use!**\"; slide 20's history step lists \"Medications: **steroids**, alpha-blockers (IFIS), **amiodarone**\"; and the deck's flash card is unambiguous — \"Q: Steroid-induced cataract pattern? A: **PSC** — Posterior SubCapsular\". Grade Gain asks exactly that at `opqb-t9-387`: the abuse of steroid eye drops leads to **posterior subcapsular cataract**.\n\n**One drug appears on both the course's list and its history checklist — amiodarone**, which is worth noting because it is a systemic cardiac drug rather than an eye drop, and because it is the only member of the list this course names twice.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Phenothiazines are cataractogenic — the course prints chlorpromazine at the head of its drug list, alongside miotics, busulfan, amiodarone and gold; beta blockers and NSAIDs are not, and the course's headline agent, the steroid, causes posterior subcapsular cataract.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.67 (Ch.9 Q24)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"An old male patient reports an improvement in his near vision and that he no longer needs his reading glasses, however, he reports that his far vision has deteriorated. Which of the following is the name of the described phenomenon?",
    options:["Presbyopia","Myopic shift","Nuclear cataract","Second sight phenomenon"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The question asks for a NAME, and `L18) Cataract II` slide 5 gives the phenomenon one, in quotation marks, under SYMPTOMS.**\n\n> **'Second sight'** — Nuclear: **myopic shift allows reading without glasses** (temporary)\n\nThat single line contains three of the four options, which is exactly why the question is harder than it looks. It names the phenomenon (*second sight*), the refractive change that produces it (*myopic shift*) and the morphology behind that (*nuclear*). The stem describes what the **patient experiences** — near vision better, distance vision worse — and the course's name for that experience is the one in quotation marks.\n\n| Option | What it actually names |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Presbyopia** | The age-related loss of accommodation, which takes **near** vision away. The stem is its reversal, which is precisely what makes the story remarkable in an old patient who already had reading glasses |\n| **B. Myopic shift** | ⚠️⚠️ **DEFENSIBLY A SECOND CORRECT NAME, AND THE PRINTED KEY IS NOT DISPUTED.** The two words sit in the same sentence of the same slide, and Grade Gain uses *myopic shift* for the same event. The distinction the deck supports is a level one: the myopic shift is the **refractive change** — \"Increased nuclear refractive index → acquired myopia\" (slide 6) — measurable on a refraction, present before any symptom; *second sight* is the **symptom** it produces once the far point reaches reading distance. This stem is written entirely in symptoms, with no refraction given, so the symptom's name is the one asked for. A reader who chose B has not misunderstood the disease |\n| **C. Nuclear cataract** | The **diagnosis**, not the phenomenon — and the answer to `opmcq-c9-2` and `opmcq-c9-5`, which ask for it in those words |\n| **D. Second sight phenomenon** | The key |\n\n**The word *temporary* is what makes this worth a question at all.** `opmcq-c9-3` puts the same story in front of a 52-year-old and asks how the ophthalmologist should react; the answer there is that it may be bad news. Second sight is a name for a stage on the way to `opmcq-c9-1`.\n\n**Cross-bank, and the discrimination is clean.** `opqb-t9-398` (a 45-year-old emmetropic driver who can read without glasses but cannot see far street signs) and `opqb-t9-407` (a 60-year-old originally emmetropic with good near reading and defective far vision) tell an almost identical story — and both ask **which type of cataract** the slit lamp will show, keying **nuclear cataract**. This House printing asks for the phenomenon's name and keys the phrase, with *nuclear cataract* demoted to a distractor. Same clinical picture, different question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Second sight is the course's name for the temporary return of unaided near vision in nuclear cataract; the myopic shift is the refractive change beneath it and nuclear cataract is the diagnosis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.67 (Ch.9 Q25; ⚠️ option B "Myopic shift" is arguably a second correct name for the same event — the printed key D stands and the overlap is explained rather than disputed)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"Which of the following is true regarding senile cataract?",
    options:["It occurs in patients above 40 years of age","It comprises 90% of cataract cases","It's either incipient, immature, mature or hypermature","All of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **THIS QUESTION AND `opmcq-c9-22` PRINT THE SAME THREE STATEMENTS IN THE SAME ORDER, ONE PAGE APART, WITH THE POLARITY FLIPPED.** There, \"All of the following are true… **except**?\" with \"D. None of the above\"; here, \"Which of the following is true…?\" with \"D. All of the above\". Both key **D**, and the two keys say the same thing: **all three statements are true**. The statements are worked through once, in full, at `opmcq-c9-22` — including the measured finding that the **90 %** figure is not printed anywhere in this course, and the deck-vs-deck divergence in which `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` prints the maturity ladder as \"immature, mature, **itumescent**, hypermature\" [sic] where `L17) Cataract I` and `L18) Cataract II` print incipient, immature, mature, hypermature.\n\n**In brief, and with the sources named:**\n\n| Statement | Source | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| Occurs above 40 years | `L17`: senile cataract \"starts from the **5th decade** onwards\" — ages 40–49 | TRUE |\n| Comprises 90% of cataract cases | Not in the course; `L18` slide 4 says only \"**Most common** — age-related\". The proportion is **not taken from the course material** | TRUE (outside the course) |\n| Incipient / immature / mature / hypermature | `L18` slide 7 \"Classification by Maturity\" is those four rows | TRUE |\n\n**Why the bank prints the pair.** The two questions are a designed exercise in reading the stem rather than the options: the option lists are nearly identical, D is the key in both, and a candidate who pattern-matches on \"D\" without noticing the word *except* will be right twice by accident and wrong the moment either question is reprinted with a false statement in it. The safe habit is to grade each statement individually and only then read option D.\n\n⚠️ **A note for the hub, not for the reader.** This is a shared-option-menu pair within one chapter, not a reprint: the stems have opposite polarity and option D differs. On the brief's rule, the **discriminating token is the word \"except\" and option D** — and unlike the ch.6 trachoma pair, the two keys here agree rather than conflict.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"All three of the bank's statements about senile cataract are true — onset in the fifth decade, the great majority of adult cataract, and the incipient / immature / mature / hypermature ladder — so the 'except' printing keys None and this printing keys All.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.67 (Ch.9 Q26; ⚠️ options A, B and C are identical, in identical order, to Ch.9 Q22 on p.66 — only option D and the absence of "except" differ, and both questions key D)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 23-year-old contact lens wearer presents with a painful red eye, photophobia, and a central corneal infiltrate. There is a small epithelial defect that stains with fluorescein. What is the most appropriate initial management?",
    options:["Start steroids immediately","Wash the eye only","Culture the ulcer, start broad-spectrum antibiotics, then modify based on sensitivity","Observe for 24 hours"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **THIS IS A CORNEA QUESTION PRINTED INSIDE THE LENS CHAPTER.** `ophthalmology MCQ.pdf` p.67 sits it between Q26 and Q28, both of which are lens questions, and nothing in it touches the lens. The bank's own chapter 8 is \"The cornea\". It is filed here to `op-cornea` — *\"Cornea — infection, trauma and the red eye\"* — by what it tests, per the chapter-filing rule, and staged in `house-c9.array.js` under the chapter it is printed in.\n\n**Every clause of the stem is a line of `L15,16) Cornea`'s bacterial keratitis block, and the key is its Clinical Pearl almost verbatim.**\n\n> **CLINICAL PEARL** — Corneal scraping should be performed in most cases **BEFORE starting antibiotics** so that the **causative organism can be cultured and its sensitivity determined**.\n\n| Stem finding | The deck's line |\n|---|---|\n| **Contact lens wearer** | \"Risk Factors: **Contact lens wear** (most common in developed world)\", and the organism that follows it — \"Gram-negative: **Pseudomonas aeruginosa** (contact lens — rapid, aggressive)\" |\n| **Pain, photophobia** | \"Symptoms: **Pain, photophobia**, blurred vision, mucopurulent discharge – acute onset\" |\n| **Epithelial defect + central infiltrate** | \"Signs: **Epithelial defect with stromal infiltrate** + circumcorneal injection\" — the defining pair |\n| **Red eye** | The circumcorneal (ciliary) injection of the same line |\n\n**The urgency is printed too, and it is what rules out waiting:** \"Severe: **Pseudomonas can cause rapid descemetocele → perforation within 24–48 h**\". A central infiltrate in a lens wearer is the exact scenario in which the organism most likely to be growing is the one that can perforate the cornea inside two days.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Start steroids immediately** | ⚠️ **Actively dangerous, and the deck flags it in capitals: \"AVOID topical steroids until infectious cause is excluded or controlled.\"** Steroids suppress the inflammatory response while the organism keeps growing. The cornea chapter's other steroid disaster is the geographical ulcer of `opmcq-c8-26`, and `opmcq-c8-22` keys topical steroids as absolutely contraindicated in a dendritic ulcer |\n| **B. Wash the eye only** | Irrigation is first aid for a chemical injury, not treatment for an established infiltrate |\n| **C. Culture the ulcer, start broad-spectrum antibiotics, then modify based on sensitivity** | The key. The three steps are the deck's investigation protocol in order — \"Corneal scraping (scalpel No.11 / 21G needle) · Gram stain · **Culture** on blood agar, chocolate agar, Sabouraud (fungal) · **ALWAYS scrape before starting antibiotics**\" — then its treatment line, \"First-line: **intensive topical fluoroquinolones** (ciprofloxacin or ofloxacin) – hourly day 1, then taper\", which is the broad-spectrum cover, and finally the sensitivity result, which narrows it |\n| **D. Observe for 24 hours** | The perforation window is 24–48 hours. Observation spends it |\n\n**One thing the option omits and the deck adds: a cycloplegic.** \"Cycloplegic drops: reduce pain from ciliary spasm, prevent synechiae\" — supportive rather than definitive, which is why it is not the answer, but it is part of the real prescription.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A contact lens wearer with a central corneal infiltrate and a staining epithelial defect has bacterial keratitis until proved otherwise: scrape and culture BEFORE antibiotics, start intensive broad-spectrum topical fluoroquinolone, then narrow by sensitivity — never steroids first, never wait.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.67 (Ch.9 Q27; ⚠️ a CORNEA question printed inside the lens chapter, between two lens questions — filed to op-cornea by what it tests)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c9-28', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"A 65-year-old man underwent cataract surgery one year ago and initially had excellent vision. He now complains of gradual blurring again, though the intraocular lens appears well centered. Slit-lamp exam shows a cloudy membrane behind the IOL. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Cystoid macular edema","Retinal detachment","Posterior capsular opacification","Endophthalmitis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The stem is `L18) Cataract II` slide 16's PCO cell written out as a case, clause for clause.**\n\n> **Months–years** · Most common late complication. **Lens epithelial cells migrate on the posterior capsule → 'after-cataract'.** Presents as **gradual return of visual blurring**. Tx: Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy — outpatient, definitive, excellent outcomes.\n\nOne year is inside \"months–years\"; \"initially had excellent vision\" then \"gradual blurring again\" is the *return* of blurring; and the cloudy membrane **behind** the IOL is the posterior capsule itself, deliberately left in place at surgery to hold the implant (slide 13: \"Posterior capsule **LEFT IN PLACE** (supports IOL)\").\n\n**\"The IOL appears well centered\" is doing real work in the stem.** Slide 16's other late implant problems are \"IOL **decentration / dislocation**\" and \"sunset/sunrise syndrome\", both of which would also blur an eye a year on — and the stem excludes them explicitly before offering the menu.\n\n| Option | Timing and picture per slide 16 | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Cystoid macular edema** | **Weeks–months**, Irvine-Gass syndrome, fluid in Henle's layer, diagnosed by OCT (petaloid pattern). Risk raised by vitreous loss, uveitis and DM | Wrong interval, and nothing would be visible behind the IOL — the lesion is at the macula |\n| **B. Retinal detachment** | Slide 16 lists it as a consequence of \"posterior capsule rupture + vitreous loss (intraop)\", so it is a real post-cataract event. But `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 11 gives rhegmatogenous detachment \"**Floaters, flashes of light, and a 'curtain' or shadow** in the peripheral vision\", progressing \"often **sudden and rapid**\" — not a gradual central blur, and not a membrane on the capsule | Wrong symptoms |\n| **C. Posterior capsular opacification** | The key |\n| **D. Endophthalmitis** | **24–72 hrs acute, or weeks delayed** — \"pain, redness, reduced VA, **hypopyon**, yellowish vitreous haze\". This eye is a year out, white and painless | Wrong interval and wrong picture |\n\n**The same complication is asked three ways across the two banks, and the discriminating word is what is being asked for.** This entry asks for the **diagnosis** from the finding; `opmcq-c9-7` asks for the **treatment** from the interval alone, with no finding given; `opqb-t9-380` supplies both the finding (\"a **grayish membrane behind the IOL**\", five years on) and asks for the treatment, keying YAG capsulotomy. `opqb-t9-349` reduces it further to which laser. And `opmcq-c9-10` keys PCO as the commonest complication of all.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A cloudy membrane behind a well-centred IOL a year after cataract surgery is posterior capsule opacification — lens epithelial cells migrating across the capsule left to support the implant, treated by Nd:YAG capsulotomy.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.67 (Ch.9 Q28, the last question of the chapter — the foot of p.67 below its answer rule is blank, and the next banner is "10. The uveal tract" on p.68)' }
